WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.480
Star Soldier, a strange and evil presence is lurking in space, crushing everything in its path, destroying spaceships full of innocent people.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.480
The brutal Star Brain is threatening the entire galactic empire.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:37.340
Star Trek, Nostalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:38.960
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.280
I'm Joe.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:41.400
And I'm Sam.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:51.920
And guys, I need to say that I was listening to a past episode and one time I didn't say chronological, I said chronological or probological.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.440
I didn't say it and nobody called me out on it and it's in the episode.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:02.400
I'm not gonna say which one, but I'm a little upset with you guys because I've been saying this for 150 plus episodes and you guys didn't notice?

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.220
We have a private group chat where we talk about the shit.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.000
You say the wrong word all the time.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.000
Like you want us to call you out every time you do that?

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.420
I say the wrong word like every five minutes on this show.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.740
You're right. I'm really bad at that.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:17.940
You almost did it right there.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.260
You almost started that wrong with us a second ago.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.240
But it also means that I'm not gonna pound you on it.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.280
Well, it's 1989 for those of you that are keeping score.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:34.200
We're on episode three of 1989 and when it's that early in the game, you're like, hey, another year.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.780
Maybe this one won't be filled with shmups.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.260
Nope. Wrong.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.280
You are. Star Soldier. Here we are.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:50.300
Guys, Star Soldier is a game that is apparently based off of Star Force, a game that we reviewed a long time ago in 1987,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:57.220
which Joe gave the opinion that it was so simple, but that was a good thing.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.940
Joe, do you think you still stand by that?

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.420
You know, I was thinking about that earlier when I revisited Star Force.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:09.220
Star Force. I guess I more meant that it was simple enough that it's just like the essence of shmups.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:16.380
So I get shmups in general aren't my thing, but I get why people might just want to play something that's like, oh, here's the base of all shmups.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:25.240
So, I mean, I think I made sort of I think I made the joke that it was like on the the factory shmup floor.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:28.840
This was the one that didn't get any of the bells and whistles to make them any different.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.320
This was just the this was the template shmup.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.180
Yeah, the economy model.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.320
Yeah, that's I guess that's sort of how I still feel about that game.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.100
You know, I went back and revisited it just just for a minute or two.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.800
So it is not exciting.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.840
It's true that there really is as bare bones as we're describing it here.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:03:06.520
Yet I this time around, I did find that somewhat satisfying in the sense that it's really fun if you're just trying to play it for like mindless shmup practice of being able to dodge enemies and shoot enemies down and stuff like that.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.220
It it didn't necessarily like change my opinion about the game at all.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:18.020
I just noticed what Joe was talking about this time where it's like, yeah, I mean, it's really simple and it's missing anything that would make it unique.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:25.740
But in a certain way, like it's kind of interesting to have something that just is like this is the genre of the game.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:31.900
You know, like imagine if Super Mario Brothers didn't have anything other than just jumping and platforms to land on.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:33.540
But you'd be like, yeah, you know what?

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.080
I'm still OK with that.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.460
Like it still is in the Super Mario Brothers engine.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.100
You know, it's just missing a lot of the interesting stuff.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:51.400
OK. And in the context of a shmup, I guess the other thing I was thinking was that it's because because there were no power ups, which I do think for me personally is a negative.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:58.540
If you're really good at shmups, you could see it as like a way to just the only way you can get further in this game is purely on skill.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.880
So, I mean, I guess that can be seen as a plus depending on what you're looking for.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.880
I think that's I think that's I think that's a little too I think it's giving it too much credit.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:11.520
I'm not but we're not here to talk about Star Force.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:19.020
I don't think that removing features is is how to make a better game by by default.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.040
I mean, that's not what what I'm saying.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.760
I'm just I don't think you're saying that.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:27.860
But I mean, it sounds like you're describing a utility application instead of a video game.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:35.340
I would say, though, that to Joe's point there, like it is kind of interesting as a as a skill thing.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:44.660
It's something you would see as a difficulty mode on a better game, you know, like something that just removes all the things that might help a player.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:53.580
Right. You can kind of go into the the debug mode, if you will, and say, like, OK, turn off all power ups, turn up, turn off all lives and stuff like that.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.900
Like, I'm just going to go into this and see how long I can survive.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.480
Star Force definitely offered that to the player.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.160
That's not very interesting for us.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.240
And it's not very interesting, like in modern gaming.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:14.960
But it's kind of neat to see to think of like an arcade game that said, you know, the only thing you have to do is dodge and shoot like that's the whole entire game.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:26.460
As Sean mentioned, though, we are here to talk about Star Soldier, a game only loosely based on Star Force.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.160
I don't want you guys to think that it's like this is a sequel.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.780
It's not. It's just this Star Star Force must have sold well.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.500
And they were like, how can we get this to be a better, more interesting shmup?

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.680
And they came up with Star Soldier in this one.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:50.600
The player pilots the starship Caesar traveling through space stations occupied by powerful supercomputers known as Star Brains who threaten the Galactic Empire.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:52.060
A couple of questions here.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:58.240
First off, does every starship need a name like the Vic Viper in Gradius?

00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.940
And then like here we have Caesar.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:06.220
Would you would you think that like, you know, like, oh, this game has no personality if the ship doesn't have a name?

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.420
I think we're in like an age of like iconic, iconic spaceships.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:17.080
The Millennium Falcon, you know, I think they want to give it a recognizable name for that reason.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:21.820
Yeah, the Enterprise. And if we're just going to go by like, do do ships have names?

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:25.620
I mean, like, I think every boat has a name unless it's like a canoe or something.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.500
So it all kind of makes sense.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:29.540
Yeah. Every spaceship has a name, too.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:36.860
Yeah. But you were never you were never calling yourself Caesar or having any kind of identification with Caesar, right?

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:39.560
Like that that's that's just a thing in the manual.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:42.240
And then you completely disregard that information, right?

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.520
You don't. Oh, yeah. You don't think of Caesar like you think of Mega Man.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:46.900
No, for sure.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:52.540
But it's also like I think that this is another one of the situations where it may as well be a direct sequel.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.840
Like there's nothing. It's not like there's a completely different plot line going on here.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.860
You're still shooting things in front of you.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.000
So to me, this is a direct sequel.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:09.920
The powerful supercomputers known as Star Brains who threaded in the Galactic Empire.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:11.580
That's straight from the manual there.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:17.740
I thought the Galactic Empire would be considered something that's bad to begin with, like they're evil.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:22.120
And then you have the Star Brains also being evil, taking down another evil.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.540
Or is this like a friendly Galactic Empire?

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:30.740
Can a Galactic Empire post Star Wars episodes four through six be considered good?

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:34.520
I see like you're in the Caesarship and it's the Empire.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:38.060
So I kind of see like Roman Empire connotations in that.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.140
I love that.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:44.260
Yeah. It really just depends on your personal view on whether imperialism is a good or bad thing.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:48.280
Yeah. And the game makes no political comment towards that.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.180
It doesn't say whether the Empire is good or bad.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.600
It challenges the player to make that distinction.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.700
Yeah. Read into our apolitical work here.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.220
This is a think piece.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:08.760
I think we can all agree that what they're trying to say is regardless, humans can be good and bad, but computers can only be bad.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.460
So supercomputers called Star Brains must be really bad.

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.540
Most likely. Because they're like trying to be humans.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:15.920
And that's our thing.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.580
Yeah. You can't have a brain.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.480
Yeah. Yeah, it was bad enough in Planet of the Apes.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.560
I can't imagine it here with supercomputers.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:27.000
So let's get into the meat and potatoes of the game.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.040
And that is the whole idea of what a shoot them up is.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:38.100
In my opinion, it's all about, you know, dodging and shooting and how that controls and stuff like that.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.060
So just gut reaction.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:46.860
Did you have a good time playing this just in the sense of how it controls and how you feel as Caesar?

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:48.960
I mean, the controls are tight.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:51.360
It's pretty fluid.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.140
You know, the better you do, the faster you are.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:58.340
So that feeling gets better, the better you play.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:08.260
Other than that, there really isn't too much more regarding the controls that I can say about it.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:18.700
Yeah. I'll say I had more fun with this than I did with Star Force, where I think they both controlled pretty well.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:23.040
Where Star Force didn't have really any bells and whistles, this added maybe like a bell and a whistle.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:24.760
So that was nice.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:29.340
Yeah, I think that the movement and the control is pretty good.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:33.700
One thing, I guess I can elaborate a little bit, that I thought was interesting.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.500
I don't really know if I like it or not, but it was interesting.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:47.520
Is that there's 3D space in this, in that you can go underneath certain parts of terrain and then resurface above certain parts of terrain.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:48.660
Which I think is cool.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.720
It's a cool way to escape enemies for a second.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:53.720
Yeah, so go further into that, Sam.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:10:02.680
Because it is a pretty unique mechanic where, you know, if you're on the above part of the terrain, you can attack everything and you can be killed by anything.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:12.600
But if you manage to glide under this surface, your ship will not be visible, but you also can't attack anything and can't be attacked by anything.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:20.720
So it's kind of like this stealth part of a shmup where you have an infinite dodge, but it's not necessarily useful because enemies continue to surround them.

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:23.960
What did you think about that in terms of actual gameplay?

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:27.020
Like on paper, it sounds great, but how did it work in the game?

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:31.180
Like, I didn't, I wasn't a huge fan of it for the most part.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:34.460
There were some parts that got a little crazy, so it was nice to duck underneath.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:42.960
But I also noticed that during those crazy parts, there's usually one of those power-ups on, or one of those items that clears all the enemies on the screen anyway.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:49.000
Somewhere on the screen that you can't shoot if you're underneath the, you know, the main stage of the game.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:59.700
So it's, I think it's an interesting option that you can, if you know that it only works when you approach things from the bottom.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:04.280
Like, I don't think you can go to the side underneath something.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:05.920
I think whatever the trigger is.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:13.060
That was going to be my point because I would just randomly do this and I had no idea how I was triggering this event.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:25.220
Like, I totally understand that this is like a risk-reward sort of thing in that if you don't want to take the risk, you're going to give up the reward of those power-ups on the top.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:31.240
And to use it as like a quick escape, I'm totally for that.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:34.040
I think that's a cool twist to shmups.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:36.300
But I don't know how I was doing it.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:39.080
I don't know if there was like a specific direction that I was going.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:42.700
Because I'm pretty sure that I did go in from the side a couple times.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:51.400
And I don't know if it's like because I wasn't holding a directional button down or if I, or because I was holding a directional button down.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:55.380
Yeah, I just couldn't get a handle on how it worked.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:58.540
See, it's funny because I felt like it was forced for me.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:08.360
Like, I felt like no matter what, if I went into these little holes that they put on the, you know, what would you even call what you're fighting through, right?

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:11.080
Terrain. If you imagine them as terrain, right?

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:12.380
There's holes in them.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:23.100
I felt like once I enter that hole, even if I didn't mean to, I was now forced to the, either the bottom or top part of the level.

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:27.760
Which is, which kind of kills the risk reward section of it.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:33.400
Because it'd be really cool if this was actually mapped to an input like, say, the B button.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.080
Yeah, because they're already duplicating the shoot button.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:40.260
So they kind of just bend something like, like if you hold this down, you will go under.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.400
And then you get to choose actually.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:45.200
Like, okay, I want to be away from all of this.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:51.860
Knowing that when I come back up, there will be possibly more enemies or I won't be able to come out of a good spot.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:55.240
Like, I'll come out and I'll be right in the middle of all the action.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:13:00.620
But being able to choose that, I think, would have been more meaningful.

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:07.660
Because by the fact that it's forced, there's a lot of times where I actually didn't want to be underground.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:09.620
And it left me worse off.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.160
Not underground, but like on the lower half of the stage.

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:16.900
It left me worse off because when I come back up, you know.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:18.760
You're surrounded. There's a lot more happening on screen.

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:20.980
Yeah. You're surrounded and you're more likely to die.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:30.520
Which in a game like this, with not that many power-ups, but you know, there's power-ups, is kind of devastating because you might have a great thing going and then you don't.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:40.500
Yeah. So I did try and test what the trigger was just by like playing the same part of the first level over and over again.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:53.360
And I'm still not certain, but it seems to me like there's literally just certain points of these surfaces that you go under and certain points that you go over every time that you hit those points.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:14:05.500
Like almost as if the surfaces are slanted or like some parts of the surfaces are higher, but they're designed in a way that when looking at them from the angle that we were looking at them, you can't tell.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:16.620
So I thought that was kind of weird because there's no indication as to what parts you're behind and which parts you're in front of until you fly into them.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:18.720
I don't know.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:29.040
It was a little strange. But also to the point that you were making, Mike, about coming out from underneath and being surrounded, I agree that that was not ideal.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:40.900
But I noticed that when I had the full power up where you're firing in all directions, rapid fire in all directions, it was a little satisfying of like hiding underneath and coming out and be like, aha!

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:44.280
And all of a sudden you're an explosion of firepower in all directions.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:47.740
But if you don't have all those, then it's definitely troublesome.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:15:00.240
And I think we've talked about in a previous Shmup episode thinking like, you know, it is kind of weird that you're on the same exact plane level as every single enemy.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.240
Like we were joking about that, that obviously it's a 2D game.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:08.120
And, you know, well, if there was some depth to it, that would be kind of cool because it's weird.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:15.740
Oh, it was 1943 we were talking about how like it's so strange that you're all the planes are exactly where you need to line up with.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:32.120
But now seeing it in practice, I don't know if I'd want like five different levels of Z-axis on a two-dimensional game where I have to like constantly shift up and down between these different platforms of the terrain to take down all the enemies.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.140
Especially one that auto-scrolls.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:40.540
I think that even when we were talking about it then, like I think in a 2D game like this, that would never have worked.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:43.820
What was the name of the space sim that we played?

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:45.540
Star Voyager.

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:51.140
I guess that would be answering the question of what would that look like on the NES.

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:54.000
Yeah. And it looked chaotic.

00:15:54.880 --> 00:16:04.420
I just played that accidentally because I thought that, Mike, you told us that this one is going to be related to Star Force and I misremembered what you told us as Star Voyager.

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:07.320
So I played Star Voyager and then realized I was supposed to be playing Star Force.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:10.100
This is not like Star Voyager.

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:12.260
I don't know why I'm going on this weird aside.

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:17.280
Star approach.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.380
Star approach.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:38.940
shot, which is like kind of funny because it reminds me how often I just hang in the

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:42.500
back of the screen, which is not like a good thing for a shmup.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.640
Like you shouldn't really hang out in the back of the screen, especially if you have

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:50.240
a shot that fires from behind because then you're not using your full power.

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:52.680
But we'll get into that in a second.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.320
I think that's a different question there.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:59.740
To continue on with the power-ups, then there's the ultimate weapon that Joe was talking about

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:04.320
where you shoot in like all, what is it, eight directions or six?

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:10.260
Okay, it's eight. You shoot in all those directions and that feels really good and really powerful.

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:17.280
I just can't help but feel like the fact that the power-up is just like that S bar that just

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:21.320
kind of floats around and you collect it and then it just continues to advance up as you

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:26.780
advance up. I couldn't help but feel like that wasn't really that exciting and removed the choice aspect

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:30.660
of power-ups that we liked so much about Gradius and Lifeforce.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:36.120
I'm not saying they had to make it a choice selection thing where you actually have to

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:40.300
go into a menu or hit the select button to choose the current power-up, but it did feel

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:45.500
less exciting that it was all just so linear and you just have to collect these power-ups

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:48.920
and they just gradually make you the next natural stage of stronger.

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:56.100
What did you think, Sean? Sean? Because it's just like more guns, like the only direction you can go in terms of how

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:57.180
powerful you get.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.400
I know you can also be a little faster, but that comes with more guns.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:06.640
If you have a turbo button, that makes this entire game trivial.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:12.880
It just, because it's just like always spewing out bullets.

