I think that that's what they want.

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:42.280
It's again, it's not for me, but like, I think that that's what, that's what makes a shmup

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:44.200
a shmup most of the time.

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:48.840
Uh, so I, I do think that that's like, that's kind of an important element of shmups.

00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:54.720
Yeah. I, I think that, um, I think it's sort of asking the same question, like with this either

00:33:54.720 --> 00:34:00.400
or thing, I mean, it's, if you need to, you need to display your skill in order to do

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:08.540
this. But if you, if you try and like learn the game, you, you will eventually do that.

00:34:08.640 --> 00:34:10.300
Like anybody can beat this game.

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:16.260
And I, I guess like what, uh, a part of your question that I can answer is, uh, whether

00:34:16.260 --> 00:34:25.180
I prefer like brute force memorization and, and Twitch reflexes or whether I, I prefer

00:34:25.180 --> 00:34:26.540
like systems mastery.

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:34.560
And if, if I, if I phrase your question in that way, I would prefer systems mastery where

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:40.200
like you need to practice the game and understand like how you play the game with the most

00:34:40.200 --> 00:34:47.240
base level, but I don't think that, uh, it would be best for it to just keep exponentially,

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:50.640
uh, getting like faster and busier.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:54.820
Uh, because I just think that that's kind of a lazy way to do difficulty.

00:34:55.240 --> 00:34:57.760
Is that sort of in the same realm of what?

00:34:58.200 --> 00:34:59.300
I think that's a very thoughtful response.

00:34:59.420 --> 00:35:00.420
Yeah. I like that.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:02.640
Sam, any, any other things to add?

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:07.560
I'm kind of in the same position as Joe, where this isn't really my genre of choice for

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:09.140
games. So I would like it.

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:13.880
I would like them in general just to be a little bit more beginner friendly just for

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:23.400
my own sake. But I also understand that these games specifically are those kinds of games where you need to

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:28.840
get used to how it plays and you need to get the reflexes and having all that is part

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:38.540
of the game. And I agree with what Sean said that it should be systems based where once you really know

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:46.760
the game inside and out, not just where enemies are going to be, but how to interact with the,

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:49.980
the game itself, like the world in the game.

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:56.080
Yeah. I think that is a good place for shmups to be.

00:35:57.580 --> 00:35:58.980
Yeah. That's awesome.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:01.160
I agree. I agree with that as well.

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:04.200
I think you guys had some great answers for a very vague question.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:06.140
And to be fair, there was no right answer.

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:13.240
I think some people would tell you that, you know, video games peaked in the early 80s with

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:19.060
these arcade formats and it was all about high scores and that skill is really like the most

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:21.800
fun part. I mean, you see that even in speed running today, right?

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:23.220
That is all about skill.

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:27.320
Like there's no, there's no room for mistakes or errors and stuff like that.

00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:31.560
And I do think, Sam, you were talking about Cadence of Hyrule last week, I think.

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:39.040
Right. And that's like, that's another game where, you know, you can't really make a ton of mistakes

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:43.800
and it is, it punishes you immediately for, for making mistakes, but you get tries over

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:45.420
and over again in the same sense.

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:49.680
Like shmups are like that, you know, they, they are not necessarily going to be a

00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:50.900
designed to be beaten.

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:53.680
They're designed to see how far you can go.

00:36:54.480 --> 00:37:07.440
Yeah. So I'm going to add some development insight from Takahashi Majin, a former Hudson Soft executive

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:09.880
and Hudson Software, the makers of this game.

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:12.820
He actually has some pretty interesting quotes here, Sean.

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:15.880
So not the, yeah, not the normal stuff.

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:21.300
He doesn't shy away from it because this is a guy who pretty much always was around the

00:37:21.300 --> 00:37:24.880
shmup scene, which in Japan, they call them STGs.

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:26.700
I'm not quite sure what that stands for.

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:30.920
Hmm. Um, but shooting themed games, who knows?

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:33.760
Uh, that sounds about as good as it can be.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:38.520
So, right. So they, they call it STGs over there, but he's been around that his whole life.

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:43.040
And when he retired, he was kind of like, yeah, these STGs nowadays suck.

00:37:43.220 --> 00:37:44.560
It's all about the older stuff.

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:48.200
And an interviewer pressed him about some of these questions.

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:49.380
So here's what he had to say.

00:37:50.260 --> 00:37:55.540
On calling shmups dodging games, he says, yeah, it seems strange to just call them all

00:37:55.540 --> 00:38:01.520
shooting games. They probably want to say that, oh, it's easy to dodge since the hitbox is so small.

00:38:01.700 --> 00:38:04.940
But I think that to the average person, it looks impossible.

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:08.560
And that has led to a great decline in the number of STG players.

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:14.540
Would you agree with that sentiment that, uh, you know, like if you look at modern shmups,

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.600
I don't know if any of you guys have seen like Ikaruga or stuff like that.

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:22.960
Would you, would you like not even try those games because they are just bullet hells that

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:29.820
look ridiculous and, and yes, you have tons of power ups and ammunition and, uh, barriers

00:38:29.820 --> 00:38:31.740
at your fingertips, but so do the enemies.

00:38:31.960 --> 00:38:39.400
No, I prefer to play those games because they seem to have more going on than what, what,

00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:43.020
what Hudson Soft has showed us these, in these couple entries.

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:46.440
And here's his thought on scoring.

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:53.720
Uh, he says, to make Lazaro, who's in Star Soldier, appear, you had to be able to tap 16

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:55.420
times in one second.

00:38:55.520 --> 00:38:59.440
So we had scoring tricks too, but they weren't essential to the game.

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:03.260
It was just, if you want to score high, you'll need to do this.

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:07.060
But you shouldn't have to pay attention to scoring to enjoy a game.

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:12.900
Just surviving and progressing through the stage, enjoying that feeling of, ah, I'm doing good.

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:14.340
I'm shooting them all down.

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:17.920
STG doesn't have to mean aiming for a high score.

00:39:18.120 --> 00:39:21.260
Kind of goes against what I was talking about just a few minutes ago, right?

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:26.660
No, I mean, I, I agree with, with that assessment, uh, because we're, we're a very anti-scoring,

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:29.040
uh, crowd here.

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:34.900
Um, so no, I mean, so I, I, I very much disagree with his first point, but I agree with that

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:36.880
one. Yeah, it makes sense, actually.

00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:40.720
I mean, I never really thought about it from like the perspective of the developer because

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:46.980
it does seem like most of these developers focus, put so much emphasis on scoring, including,

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:51.360
you know, in this game. But, uh, but not, yeah, when I think about it, you're right.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:54.240
You can, you can do, you can totally just do what we are doing.

00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:59.680
And I'm sure they had that in mind of just ignoring the score, but then, you know, certain

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:01.880
things like Sam, there's something you were talking about earlier.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:06.100
Oh, the eyes like won't mean anything to us because it doesn't matter if you shoot them

00:40:06.100 --> 00:40:07.740
because he cares about the points.

00:40:07.900 --> 00:40:14.820
And then one more from, uh, Takahashi, he says on players here, that was because the basics

00:40:14.820 --> 00:40:16.160
were all still there.

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:19.520
STGs are the simplest kind of games to make.

00:40:19.520 --> 00:40:24.220
So it's not as if they'll ever completely vanish, but unfortunately it's not as if they'll

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:26.840
it's no longer a genre that the masses care much about.

00:40:27.080 --> 00:40:33.120
The impression most people have is that this is a genre of games only people with skills

00:40:33.120 --> 00:40:38.780
can play. I, that, that, that's, I don't think he's wrong in that assessment.

00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:47.940
Well, I mean, I guess the, the, the similarities between his opinion on this, uh, on this comment

00:40:47.940 --> 00:40:49.700
as opposed to his first one.

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:54.600
Um, he's saying that like, you know, the whole genre is sort of fallen by the wayside here.

00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:57.540
And, uh, he is right in that assessment.

00:40:57.560 --> 00:41:00.280
It's, it's a very, uh, niche thing.

00:41:00.820 --> 00:41:07.600
Um, but I, I, I think he's, he's sort of acting like one of those guys that like, oh, if they

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:13.080
just, if they were all just purists, uh, everything would, would still be in the heyday.

00:41:13.780 --> 00:41:21.360
And I don't think that just because a genre has fallen into, uh, kind of the, uh, into,

00:41:21.480 --> 00:41:25.640
into more of like a niche market like that doesn't mean that there are any worse.

00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:32.500
I mean, just because they got more, the people that are interested in them are really interested

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:37.800
in them now. Um, I, I guess I've sort of lost my point, but.

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:39.260
Well, no, I hear what you're saying though.

00:41:39.700 --> 00:41:45.100
To, to, to maybe pick up at least where I think, uh, you were, you might've been going

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:51.300
with that. I feel like if anything, that could make these games better because it allows the developers

00:41:51.300 --> 00:41:53.420
to focus on what those people want.

00:41:53.420 --> 00:41:57.660
So, you know, instead of focusing on what's everybody going to love, what's going to be

00:41:57.660 --> 00:42:01.460
appealed to a broad audience, like they can get very specific about the things that people

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:04.460
who play shmups like, and they can continue to build on those things.

00:42:05.440 --> 00:42:09.420
That's what Pokemon fans have been wanting for a long time is like, stop appealing to

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:10.620
the broad player base.

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:13.620
And just like, I play this Pokemon game for 20 years.

00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:14.880
Give me some respect.

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:19.660
But yeah, that's, that's, I think what you were getting at as well, Sean.

00:42:19.660 --> 00:42:23.740
Um, I don't know if you quite finished the thought there, but like, it's strange that

00:42:24.880 --> 00:42:31.520
he's asking for games to go back to like star soldier levels of shoot them up where it's

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:33.300
like, you know, things used to be a little more simple.

00:42:33.460 --> 00:42:36.700
It used to be more just about shooting the enemies and, and dodging.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:40.600
And now they've added all this flashiness and it's become really hardcore.

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:46.720
And there's too many power ups and too many different types of enemies and, and too many

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:48.460
stages. It sounds very crotchety to me.

00:42:48.660 --> 00:42:51.260
Right. It's like, I don't know if I'd want to go back to this.

00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:54.920
Yeah. As I don't know, the games are too fun now.

00:42:55.460 --> 00:42:59.500
Yeah. We got all this cool, interesting stuff in them.

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:05.680
It'd be like if people told, uh, Naughty Dog, like stay away from the last of us and go

00:43:05.680 --> 00:43:06.980
back to Crash Bandicoot.

00:43:07.120 --> 00:43:08.720
Right? Yeah. Like. Mm-hmm.

